reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
Chance sources:
- the deal of contract colours, which is symmetric, since each colour has exactly 6 cards and all are revealed;
- the deck order, which decides whether your colour lands in rounds where you hold early priority, or on the same round as another player's colour.

Estimate. Each player sees about 6 own-colour lots. A swing of 1-2 contested lots is worth about 5-10 points out of a winning score of about 40-50. I put chance at roughly 25-35% of the outcome.

When all players play well (own-colour limit 5, scrap limit 1, denial bids of 2 against the inferred leader), the result depends on:
- inferring opponents' colours from their public purchases and queue choices;
- choosing which 2 of the 3 lots to queue on, given the known priority order;
- deciding when a denial at price 2 helps you rather than the third player;
- reading the talk as possibly bluffed.

The deck matters, but it is not the sole decider. There is real hidden information (colours), visible signals (who queued where and what each paid), and a way to act on it (denial queues and lot selection). A player who reasons well is clearly favoured over a naive bidder, whom the policies above show losing.
